  "issues": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "identification": "Vol. 1 No. 1 (2026): Hermeneutical Openings",
      "volume": 1,
      "number": "1",
      "year": 2026,
      "published": true,
      "published_date": "2025-12-31 00:00:00",
      "doi": "10.21423/btd23k20",
      "url": "https://gadamer-ojs-tamu.tdl.org/gadamer/issue/view/1",
      "description": "<p data-start=\"324\" data-end=\"556\">This is the inaugural issue of <em data-start=\"357\" data-end=\"389\">The Journal of Gadamer Studies</em>. Volume 1, issue 1 features a newly translated essay by Gadamer on the poet Hilde Domin as well as articles exploring themes such as the hermeneutics of prayer, Gadamer’s philosophy of art, metaphysical hermeneutics, the immediacy of experience, and Gadamer’s dialogue with Parmenides. Also included is a book symposium on <em data-start=\"938\" data-end=\"986\">The Event of Meaning in Gadamer’s Hermeneutics</em> and a review of <em data-start=\"1007\" data-end=\"1052\">Gadamer and the Social Turn in Epistemology</em>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "identification": "Vol. 1 No. 2 (2026): Language, Virtue, Friendship and Communal Practice",
      "volume": 1,
      "number": "2",
      "year": 2026,
      "published": true,
      "published_date": "2026-10-22 16:07:08",
      "doi": "10.21423/qjavje31",
      "url": "https://gadamer-ojs-tamu.tdl.org/gadamer/issue/view/3",
      "description": "<p>Volume 1, issue 2 of <em>The Journal of Gadamer Studies</em> features a new English translation of Gadamer’s “Hermeneutics and Theology” (1977), accompanied by an introductory essay. In his essay, Gadamer examines theology as a human science, exploring the distinctive character of religious discourse, scriptural interpretation, and the relation between theological reflection, faith, and lived communal practice. The introduction explicates these themes and considers their significance for contemporary philosophy of religion and hermeneutical scholarship.</p>\r\n<p>The issue also brings together contributions examining the linguistic, ethical, anthropological, and historical dimensions of Gadamer’s thought. “Mental Language and Life’s Self-Movement” explores Gadamer’s distinctive reworking of the Christian doctrine of the inner word (<em>verbum interius</em>). “Hermeneutic Virtue: From the Common to the Fecund” examines Gadamer’s conception of hermeneutic virtue as an expansion of practical understanding within social life. It draws on Aristotle’s account of practical wisdom and friendship and explores how such understanding counters the struggle for recognition and its inherent inequality while opening life to its fecundity. “The Humanist Roots and Anthropological Valences of Gadamer’s Hermeneutics” traces Gadamer’s intellectual inheritance from Renaissance humanism and argues that philosophical hermeneutics entails an implicit conception of the human being. “Hans-Georg Gadamer: Philosopher, Teacher, Friend” offers a personal and philosophical reflection on Gadamer’s relationship with Heidegger, taking as its point of departure the author’s translation of the Rilke poem Gadamer placed at the beginning of <em>Truth and Method</em>. Issue 2 concludes with a German-language review of the book, <em>Sprache und Außersprachlichkeit,</em> which examines the relation between language and what exceeds it, thus raising questions about the limits of understanding and the possibilities of non-understanding.</p>"
    }
  ],
